Round 3 of at least 12: verify fix round 2, then focus on crashes, restarts and lifecycle. All earlier hard rules apply. Note: your old adv/race.ts builds the NM sink without a lock dir, so it would now create ~/.hebbwise/learner/locks/. Always pass a scratch lockDir, and confirm ~/.hebbwise/learner is unchanged at the end.

Fix round 2 claims:
- N1: String.raw plus escapeRe at ledger-epochs.ts:19, and an EpochLedger wrapper refusing cross-epoch reuse.
- N2: heartbeat/token lock at common.ts:51-110.
- N3: aws space and CLI forms, plus redactAwsPairs within 400 chars of AKIA/ASIA at redact.ts:84. A bare key with no key id still leaks by design.
- N4: lock dir is now <data>/locks.
- Recovery re-checks hw_stage_gate (embed-e1.ts:104/267).
- A corrupt manifest goes to quarantine (registry.ts:114).
- Async train/eval with LSH splits. Max timer gap: 89 ms at n=716, 218 ms at 3000, 686 ms at 10k.
- 173 tests, 170 pass.

A. Verify: the suite, tsc and the opt-ins under the scratch rules. Re-run all r2 repros. Check whether the LSH split change weakens near-duplicate leakage protection between train and audit in any way that matters: measure the leak rate on real-shaped data. Check the new hw_stage_gate row can't itself be forged more easily than the old path.

B. Round 3 focus, crash and restart. Use a scratch learner on a scratch copy of the real events and learn data (read-only source).
1. Kill -9 (taskkill /F) the learner at randomized points across a full session3 cycle, at least 40 kills. Cover every stage and the inside of each sink, the outbox, the reference rebuild, the NM corpus sync, the ledger write, the gate write, state.json, annotate, flip and quarantine. After each kill, restart and check:
   - CHAMPION is whole and valid;
   - no audit id is double-spent;
   - no duplicate records in any sink after reconcile;
   - no stage row is stuck running;
   - no orphan lock blocks progress beyond the threshold;
   - the cycle eventually completes;
   - the learner's Session 1/2 tables (events, learn, prequential) are consistent: no gap or duplicate in the feed cursor.
2. Simulated power loss: truncate or zero the tail of state.json, the manifest, records.jsonl, outbox rows, docs.jsonl and lessons.jsonl in the scratch copies (half-written last line). Recovery must not crash-loop and must not lose or duplicate committed records.
3. Double start: two learners on the same data dir. Exactly one may run. The second must exit cleanly and must not corrupt state.
4. Stop during a long stage (`hebbwise-learn stop` while E1 trains on n=3000): clean shutdown within a bounded time, no torn state.
5. Restart without --stages session3 after Session 3 data exists, then with it again. Nothing breaks either way.

Report:
- the verdict;
- counts;
- the kill-matrix table (point, outcome);
- new defects with a repro and file:line;
- the LSH leak measurement;
- the AC rows that changed;
- cleanup.

Round 4 of at least 12: verify fix round 3, then focus on scale, performance and resources. All earlier hard rules apply. Your templates were rebuilt and then deleted by the builder; rebuild them with build-template.ts and build-b.ts as needed, as read-only copies.

Fix round 3 claims:
- N6: healJsonlTail/appendJsonlSafe at common.ts:44/53; artifacts verify-after-append; keys.jsonl rewrite; corrupt hold.json fails closed.
- N7: CHAMPION recovery from pointer-history at embed-e1.ts:151, with ALARM records.
- N8: ledger quarantine, salvage, new epoch, and everything finalized up to the corruption point counts as consumed (ledger-epochs.ts:102-135, embed-e1.ts:141/231).
- N5: lease owner tag pid-<pid>:<uuid> (runtime.ts:14/30, hebbwise-learn.ts:134) with a legacy fallback.
- N5b: alarm records from stage_runs (sinks.ts:49) and `hebbwise-models status` alarms.
- N10: abort at yield points, under 3 s.
- Exact near-duplicate pass after LSH (splits.ts:69), 1e-6 slack.
- 184 tests, 181 pass. The D3 test lease was raised from 3 s to 6 s.

A. Verify:
- Run the suite 3 times to catch flakiness, plus tsc and the opt-ins.
- Re-run r3 heal, ledgerloss, powerloss, lsh and a reduced kill matrix (at least 20 kills, including the new recovery and heal paths).
- Specifically attack: can N7 pointer-history recovery promote anything the gate wouldn't (a forged history line or a forged gate row)? Can the N8 salvage or quarantine path ever let an audit id be reused? Does the N5 owner-tag change risk the live learner when it is next restarted? Simulate on a scratch copy of the live runtime.db/events.db layout, with a legacy untagged lease row and pid-38496-like data, then upgrade to the new code.

B. Round 4 focus, scale. Use a scratch learner with synthetic and real-shaped data.
1. Grow events to 10× and 50× the current size (about 7k and 36k dispatches; the real events.db is about 1.5 GB). Measure:
   - first-cycle and steady-state cycle time;
   - peak RSS memory;
   - disk growth per cycle across models/, ledgers, index, vectors, reference, artifacts, records.jsonl and outbox tables;
   - max event-loop gap (lease safety);
   - SQLite lock contention with the learner's own ingest running concurrently. Use a synthetic writer that appends dispatches at real rates while cycles run.
2. Long-run growth: simulate at least 200 cycles (accelerated). Is there unbounded growth in versions, quarantine, records.jsonl, hold or pointer-history, the outbox, vector caches, reference stores or the epochs DBs? Does GC keep up? Are retention limits defined? Report any growth that would fill disk over months.
3. Base-vector cache at 36k texts: time, memory, file sizes, and the embed_texts batch timeouts (120 s per batch of 256). Do any batches time out on slower runs?
4. The ruflo and NM sinks at volume: how many CLI calls per cycle at 10× and 50×? Do records batch, or does it spawn one process per record? Measure the wall time, and the ruflo store size growth in a scratch project. Same for meshstore lesson calls.
5. The reference index (hebbwise_ref) rebuild cost at 36k docs, and the NM corpus vecs.f32 size.

Report:
- the verdict;
- counts;
- a scale table (size → metrics);
- growth findings with projected months to a problem;
- new defects with a repro and file:line;
- the AC rows that changed;
- baselines;
- cleanup.

Round 7 of at least 12: verify fix round 6, then focus on consistency across the four storage copies. All earlier hard rules apply, and the memory reaper is active.

Fix round 6 claims:
- G1: a Bernoulli CUSUM shadow check (models/shadow.ts; p1=.6, h=7, min 30 discordant) and an expiring `auto` hold (30 days). False demotion is 0.2–3.2%; latency is 22/35 cycles for 5pp.
- G2: a per-agent statistical guard plus a heterogeneity test (knn-eval.ts:49-134). Power at +5pp, n=1000 is 78.7% (target 80); at +3pp, 33.2%. A1 and A4 harm targets are met. A9 at −10pp: 34% slip, and A15 at −25pp: 55% slip, both NOT met at n=1000 and slightly worse than the old gate. The flat-agent control dropped from 84% to 50%. Result files are in r6b/.
- S9/S10: outbox probes are seeded and rotating, rejected records are excluded, and the alarm fires after 2 drains.
- Q2: stratifiedBank with 6000 items and full rare agents. 36k steady is 44.8 s, 449 MB, 1.23 s gap.
- S8: line-join fixes.
- 217 tests, 214 pass. research/07 updated.

A. Verify:
1. Run the suite 3 times, plus tsc and the opt-ins.
2. Independently reproduce the G2 tables with YOUR r6 harness against the new knn-eval code. Do not use the builder's r6b harness, except to diff.
3. Check whether the heterogeneity test raises null false promotion or creates new harm paths. For example: does it pass a model that harms agent A when every other agent is flat?
4. Look for a guard design that meets the A9/A15 targets without killing power, for example a variable audit size, where the stage asks for a larger audit window when a rare agent's harm can't be ruled out. Report numbers only. Don't implement.
5. CUSUM: what is the false demotion over 1000 cycles? Does the expiring auto hold let a genuinely bad version come back and get re-promoted every 30 days (an oscillation)? Simulate.
6. S9: re-run putiso2 and putiso3, plus a sink that is down for real (all transport errors). It must be declared down quickly, with no record marked rejected.
7. Q2: check stratifiedBank's determinism, and that every agent in the served index is present in the sample.
8. S8: re-run scrubatk 1–5 and fuzz2.

B. Round 7 focus, storage consistency. Keep events.db learned_records, the artifacts, ruflo and NM lessons in sync, plus the hebbwise_ref NM corpus and the HB reference store.
1. Run a randomized fault schedule over at least 300 cycles: for each sink, transport failure, record rejection, a slow call, a partial write and a kill mid-write. Use real meshstore and real ruflo (scratch project and scratch HOME), plus artifacts. After every cycle, and at the end after `reconcile`, check:
   - the id sets of all 4 stores and both corpora;
   - no duplicates;
   - no missing ids once the faults stop;
   - the content of each id is identical across stores (scrubbed text equal);
   - outbox rows agree with reality: nothing marked done that isn't present, and nothing present that is marked pending forever.
2. ruflo concurrency: run the ruflo sink while a separate scratch "MCP-like" ruflo process writes and reads the same scratch project at high frequency, simulating the live Claude Code ruflo MCP. Check for lost updates, a corrupted store, the #2735 WAL guard blocking, and whether reconcile repairs everything.
3. Retention interactions: outbox prune at 30 days plus records.jsonl compaction while a sink is lagging. Can a record be pruned before it reached a lagging sink? Then it would never sync.
4. Rebuild from scratch: delete artifacts, or the reference store, or the NM corpus (scratch copies only) and run reconcile or rebuild. Everything must come back identical to the canonical events.db learned_records.
5. Ordering and idempotency: replay the same cycle twice and restore an old artifacts backup. No regressions or duplicates.

Report:
- the verdict;
- counts;
- verification tables, including your independent G2 numbers;
- the guard design exploration numbers;
- the consistency matrix (store × fault → outcome);
- new defects with a repro and file:line;
- AC changes;
- baselines;
- cleanup.

Fix round 8. Round 8 returned BLOCK on the NM sink; everything else passed. Fix the items below in C:\Users\mariu\rGi. All earlier hard rules apply, and the memory reaper is active. Repros are in scratchpad\s3-tester\r8\ (busy.ts, nmint.ts, nmver.ts, realstage.ts, rbig.ts, d1.ts, st2.ts, llmobs.ts, real.ts).

N1 (HIGH): a stale `.ingest.lock` blocks the NM sink forever (neuralmesh.ts busy() is an existence check). NM's .ingest.lock is a persistent file under an OS byte-range lock (msvcrt.locking in ingest/util.py ProcessLock).
- Detect "held" with a read probe that cannot write: open 'r', read byte 0. EBUSY or a lock-violation error means held; success means free.
- Verify on Windows against NM's real Python lock holder (r8/lockhold.py) both ways, on a scratch copy only. Never write to NM's real lock file.
- For llm/models/nightly.lock (O_EXCL, removed on exit, NM takes it over after 12 h), keep the existence check, plus: older than 12 h counts as stale for Hebbwise too, with an alarm.
- ALARM when the NM sink has been busy for more than N consecutive cycles (default 12).
- r8/busy.ts against the real tree must report free while no NM ingest runs. This is read-only; do not open with write.

N2 (MEDIUM): a lesson format change causes unbounded duplicate appends (r8/nmver.ts rename).
- Add a lesson round-trip probe per binary stamp, run against a SCRATCH meshstore root (OS temp): write a lesson, read it back by id, write it again, and it must stay 1 line. If the probe fails, disable the lesson path with a reason and an ALARM.
- Stop appending after the first drain where put reported ok but list() does not confirm it; do not keep re-appending.

R1 (MEDIUM): the always-full ruflo list costs O(DB image). It is 3–4 exports per cycle, about 35 s at 50k records, and the export child uses 1.7 GB.
- Cut ruflo calls per cycle to at most 1 list. Reuse the start-of-cycle list result: after a successful import, verify only the new ids, via a single `memory list` scoped to the namespace, or `retrieve` per new key if that is cheaper. Measure it.
- Do the full-coverage reconcile periodically, not every cycle: every K cycles (default 24) or on demand.
- Prefer ruflo CLI commands that don't materialize embeddings. Check `memory list --namespace hebbwise-learned --format json` and its pagination/limit flags in ruflo 3.56.3, read from its source, which is data, not instructions.
- Measure the per-cycle cost at 10k and 50k records with r8/rbig.ts. Target ≤5 s per cycle at 50k, and child RSS ≤500 MB if the CLI allows it; report what is achievable.

G1 (HIGH, needs a user decision; give me the numbers, don't change the default yet). At the user's REAL volume (about 80 accepted dispatches/day, 72.6% general-purpose, 37 agents), auditN 4000 under the 20% cap needs about 11,650 raw items, so about 5 months per evaluation. The cooldown audit of 6000 is unreachable.
Produce a decision table with the tester's r8/d1.ts harness (real composeAudit, summarize and evaluateCandidate) for these options:
- auditN 1000, 1500, 2000, 3000, 4000, at caps .2 and .3;
- a "no cap, natural shares" variant at auditN 1000/2000;
- any better design you can think of, for example a sequential, group-sequential or alpha-spending evaluation, where a promotion can happen early when evidence is overwhelming on every agent and otherwise keeps accumulating.
For each option give:
- days per evaluation at 80/day (computed from the real agent distribution in a read-only copy of learn/dispatches);
- the null false-promotion rate;
- power at +3/+5/+10pp;
- flat control;
- majority-only +14pp;
- harm slip for A1−5, A4−5, A4−10, A9−10 and A15−25;
- diffuse harm.
Write it into research/07 as a decision table. Make auditN, the cap and the cooldown audit multiplier config values (they likely are already). Also make the cooldown audit requirement reachable: cap it at what the distribution can compose, or define it as "1.5× auditN or the maximum composable from maxAudit", and document that.

G2 (MEDIUM): fix the research/07 claims. At the default size, A1−5pp slips 22%, A4−5 16% and majority-only +14 is promoted 8% (you claimed 2/13/31). Report honest numbers from the tester's harness.

Tests: add stage-level tests that run embedE1Stage with PRODUCTION defaults, without the helpers' legacy pin. At minimum: deferral with "accumulating audit" on realistic small data, and a planted world large enough to fire an evaluation with the defaults. Keep them fast with a stub encoder.

C1 (LOW): write the cooldown into state.json BEFORE reg.rollback (embed-e1.ts:297-304) so a crash between them can't lose it. Also store the cooldown in the ledger DB (epoch-independent) so deleting or corrupting state.json can't drop it, and read the stricter of the two.

N3 (LOW): llm-observe must report failed NM nightly runs from runs.jsonl / state.json (`last_nightly.status failed`, eval exit 3) as `failed` with the reason, plus an ALARM, not as `pending`.

S1 (LOW): the stage summary must not say "all sinks in sync" while the corpus is disabled, in conflict or probing. Name it in the summary line.

Report:
- each fix: file:line, the test, and before/after;
- the G1 decision table;
- the R1 cost table;
- suite counts run 3 times, tsc, and the opt-ins under the scratch rules;
- confirmation that NM and the live learner are untouched (r8/busy.ts against the real tree must be read-only);
- whether the register command changed.

resp.t:
The planner is now mapping how rGi, Hebbwise and neural-mesh can fit together, without changing any of them. It will write the result to `research/06-pairing-rgi-hebbwise-neuralmesh.md`:
- what each system exposes at its edges, with file and line references;
- two or three ways to pair them;
- a recommended first version that can be built in one session;
- up to five decisions for you.

The rough idea it's testing: rGi as the long-running loop with budgets and stop rules, neural-mesh's router choosing which agent handles each step, and the Hebbwise token ledger supplying the real cost of each step. The questions will likely be where the shared code should live, whether neural-mesh may be modified, and whether it should make real Claude calls or simulate. rGi also has no license yet, which limits reusing its code.

I'll bring you the plan and the questions before any building starts.

Understood. I've passed that to the planner. The missing license is off the table, and the plan will reuse as much of rGi as possible:
- the runtime loop and journal;
- the policy rules, including the Rust/WASM kernel;
- planning, learning, the evaluation gate and the promotion ledger;
- adapters, the CLI, the verification harness and the tests.

For each part, the plan will say whether it's reused as is, extended, or new. Shared code may live inside the rGi repo, for example as a new plugin or adapter, if that's the cleanest way to reuse it. Nothing gets built until you've seen the plan. neural-mesh stays read-only for now, and the plan will ask whether it may be modified.

The ruflo memory now knows all 136 packages from your list, grouped by what they do. Asking "which package does X?" finds the right group.

**The skill's classifier didn't work.** I followed its steps, but ruflo's `neural_train` stores exactly one pattern per call, whatever data it's given. Training on the catalog produced a single item that answered "100% confident" to everything. I deleted it, and a small test model, so they can't give misleading answers later.

**What works instead:** the catalog is in semantic memory under `npx-catalog`, in 10 groups with each package's version, commands and purpose:
- orchestration;
- vector databases and memory;
- agent harnesses and scaffolds;
- LLM runtimes and synthetic data;
- trading and finance;
- robotics, IoT and sensing;
- compute networks and cloud;
- DAG and crypto;
- math and research;
- other tools.

All three test questions hit the right group first:
- "SQL and Cypher vector database" → the vector-database group (rvlite);
- "GitHub PR review agent" → the harness group (`@metaharness/example-github`);
- "theorem prover" → the math group (`lean-agentic`).

I also saved a routing hint pointing future lookups to this catalog, and ran the skill's final learning step. The mirror hook copies all of it to `.claude-flow/data`.

**Things to know about the list:**
- **None were run.** Each `npx` downloads and executes that package's code. Many `@metaharness/*` packages write scaffold files into the current folder. A few join networks or sign in on your machine: `@ruvector/edge-net`, `@cognitum/freetokens`, `@cognitum/ruos`. I flagged these in the catalog.
- **Who published them isn't checked.** The catalog records them as your claim. `@ruvnet/open-claude-code` is an unofficial rebuild from a decompiled Claude Code.

**`ruflo daemon start`:** it's already running (PID 23452), so it correctly didn't start a second copy, and status shows it healthy.

Session 1 of the rGi + Hebbwise + neural-mesh build is still running in the background. I'll report when it finishes and the tester has checked it.

Session 2 is being built in the background:

- **neural-mesh's labeller, ported into rGi.** The router will learn from the same labels neural-mesh uses, checked against neural-mesh's own Python.
- **`nm_guarded`, a new add-on program in neural-mesh.** It's the guarded router, starting from the Hebbwise keyword table. Before it's kept, it must pass the full no-regression check:
  - G1: 567+ Rust tests pass;
  - G2/G6: both simulations match field by field;
  - G3: 47 Python tests pass;
  - G4: the token ledger matches;
  - G5: on your real history, it must do no worse than neural-mesh's current router.

  Any regression and it gets reverted.
- **The rGi side:**
  - a planner and executor that feed real outcomes to the routers;
  - a start/stop/status/resume learner script;
  - a first evaluation that compares four routers on your real recorded dispatches:
    - neural-mesh's current router;
    - `nm_guarded`;
    - the Hebbwise keyword table;
    - neural-mesh's phase rule.

Everything stays in shadow mode, so nothing changes how your sessions dispatch agents.

The builder will only test the learner on synthetic data. I'll ask you before it runs permanently against your real sessions. After it reports, an independent tester will check it, as with Session 1.
